+# Deploying the origin migration
+
+The supplied app is a static page with no existing install manifest or stored user configuration. These additions prepare two deployments; they do not move an existing installation until both origins are configured and deployed.
+
+1. Set `oldAppUrl`, `newAppUrl`, `oldAppId`, and `newAppId` in `migration.config.json` to absolute HTTPS URLs. The two origins must share a registrable domain (including private suffix rules). Use the **actual installed app's existing manifest ID** for `oldAppId`; if its manifest omitted an ID, use its resolved `start_url`. Do not invent a new predecessor identity.
+2. List the exact localStorage keys holding user preferences in `settingsKeys`. This repository currently stores none, so the list starts empty. Only listed keys are copied; existing values on the destination are replaced. Never list authentication tokens or other secrets. IndexedDB, cookies, server account settings, and permissions need their own application-specific migration; they are not handled here.
+3. Run `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`, then `pnpm build`. To use a separate configuration file, set `MIGRATION_CONFIG` to its path. The build refuses unconfigured or cross-site destinations rather than emitting a live authorization for example domains.
+4. Deploy `dist/new` at the new origin root, and `dist/old` at the old origin root. Retain the old manifest's existing name, icons, scope, display settings, and other integration members when applying these generated migration fields to a previously installed app. The included coffee icon is only a starter asset; use the installed app's original production icons.
+5. Serve `.well-known/web-app-origin-association` as JSON at the **old origin root**, without authentication or redirects. Include dotfiles in deployment. Serve manifests as `application/manifest+json`. Keep both manifests and the old app URLs available without redirects during the rollout, with short cache lifetimes.
+6. Keep `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy` compatible with cross-origin popup messaging on the old page and new `transfer.html` (for example, `unsafe-none`). A policy that severs `window.opener` prevents the settings handover. The transfer page must be served over HTTPS and must remain on the configured new origin.
+7. Users select **Move my settings** on the old app before accepting the browser's installation update. The transfer opens a new-origin page, copies the allowlisted values, waits for a save acknowledgement, and leaves the old values intact. A failed or timed-out transfer can be retried. Do not retire the old origin until users have had time to transfer their settings.
+
+The generated new manifest declares `migrate_from`; the old manifest supplies `migrate_to` as an object, and the association authorizes the destination's **full manifest ID**. These follow [Chrome's current origin migration documentation](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/seamless-pwa-origin-migration). Chrome 150 introduced same-site migration. Supported browsers offer an installation update to preserve OS integration. Other browsers receive a visible link and reinstall instructions; a website cannot preserve their existing OS shortcuts automatically. Notification and other permissions remain origin-bound and require a new grant.
+
+Run `pnpm test` for non-browser checks. No dev server or browser is required. The default `start` script still serves the original source page; deploy the generated role-specific output for migration.
